\f0\fs24 \cf2 2. \
\
Bag-of-words and embedding-based representations involve important trade-offs in interpretability and robustness for downstream modeling.\
\
First, interpretability differs substantially. Bag-of-words representations preserve a direct link between words and model outputs: each feature corresponds to an observed term, making coefficients easy to interpret and validate. For example, in a study of parliamentary speeches on foreign policy, a document\'96term matrix allows researchers to directly test whether the presence of words like \'93deterrence\'94 or \'93alliance\'94 predicts hawkish voting behavior. In contrast, embedding-based representations (such as Word2Vec or transformer embeddings) encode documents as dense numerical vectors whose dimensions do not correspond to identifiable words. While this makes interpretation difficult, embeddings capture semantic relationships that bag-of-words cannot, such as recognizing that \'93military intervention\'94 and \'93use of force\'94 convey similar meanings even when they share no words.\
\
Second, the two approaches differ in robustness and generalization. Bag-of-words models are sensitive to exact vocabulary choices and may fail when texts use different wording across contexts or time periods. Embedding-based models are more robust because they rely on contextual similarity rather than exact token overlap. For example, when analyzing protest demands across countries, an embedding-based model can learn that phrases like \'93government accountability,\'94 \'93regime transparency,\'94 and \'93anti-corruption reforms\'94 express related political claims, even if no single keyword appears consistently. This makes embeddings particularly useful in cross-national or multilingual research where linguistic variation is substantial.\
\
In practice, I would prefer bag-of-words representations when the research goal is hypothesis testing with transparent mechanisms, such as estimating how specific frames or keywords affect public opinion or elite behavior. I would prefer embedding-based representations when studying latent political meaning or performing prediction across heterogeneous texts, such as classifying ideology in social-media posts or identifying substantively similar policy positions across international documents where wording varies widely.\
\
4.5\
\
The LDA model identifies several interpretable topics from the movie plot corpus. One prominent topic is centered on war and violence, with high-probability terms such as 
\f1\i \cf2 war, army, soldiers, military,
\f0\i0 \cf2  and 
\f1\i \cf2 attack
\f0\i0 \cf2 , indicating narratives focused on armed conflict and combat. Another topic emphasizes family and interpersonal relationships, reflected by terms like 
\f1\i \cf2 father, mother, family, home,
\f0\i0 \cf2  and 
\f1\i \cf2 life
\f0\i0 \cf2 , which capture domestic and relational storylines common in drama films. Preprocessing choices play a key role in shaping topic quality: removing English stopwords prevents topics from being dominated by function words, while setting min_df = 5 reduces noise from rare or idiosyncratic terms. Restricting tokens to alphabetic words with at least two letters further improves coherence by excluding punctuation and malformed tokens. However, these choices may also remove infrequent but substantively meaningful words, illustrating a trade-off between topic interpretability and coverage.\
\
6.4\
\
Compared to LDA, BERTopic produces topics that are more semantically coherent but less immediately interpretable in terms of explicit word distributions. LDA represents topics as probability distributions over words, which makes topic interpretation straightforward by inspecting top words, while BERTopic clusters documents based on contextual embeddings and summarizes topics post hoc. BERTopic is less sensitive to surface-level preprocessing choices such as stopword removal, because semantic embeddings capture meaning beyond exact token overlap, whereas LDA is highly sensitive to vocabulary construction and frequency thresholds. However, BERTopic is substantially more computationally expensive, requiring transformer inference, dimensionality reduction, and density-based clustering, while LDA scales efficiently to large corpora. In this dataset, BERTopic identifies many fine-grained semantic themes and explicitly flags ambiguous documents as outliers, whereas LDA assigns every document to a topic regardless of fit. BERTopic is preferable when capturing nuanced semantic similarity or implicit themes is important, while LDA may be preferable for large-scale exploratory analysis where interpretability and computational efficiency are priorities.}